-- Florida. The state had two stops, and both were duplicates of entries in
-- this file, so it effectively had none.
--
-- The best-sourced batch yet: floridastateparks.org and nps.gov are the top
-- two domains and aggregators barely appear. That is what asking for the
-- official page during research rather than afterwards produces.
--
-- Two slug collisions, both caught by the check that was missing during the
-- six-state import and is now part of the parser. Spook Hill would have
-- overwritten the one at Wappingers Falls, New York, and Medieval Torture
-- Museum the one in Chicago — each fifteen hundred miles from the stop it
-- would have silently rewritten. Neither is anywhere near the other
-- geographically, so proximity would never have found them.
--
-- Skunk Ape Research Headquarters and Spook Hill are dropped: both are
-- already here from the OpenStreetMap scan.
--
-- ACCESS WAS INFERRED, not supplied. This file arrived in a different format
-- with no access column, so each value was worked out from the name, the
-- category and the source domain: anything on floridastateparks.org or nps.gov
-- is open, museums and forts and lighthouses are limited, statues and markers
-- are roadside. Six were corrected by hand where the rule was plainly wrong —
-- Spook Hill is a public street, the Devil's Chair is a bench in a cemetery.
--
-- The default when no rule matched was 'limited', chosen deliberately: telling
-- somebody to check first is a smaller error than sending them to a locked
-- gate. It is still a guess on about forty-five of these and worth a pass.
--
-- 223 stops, every one with a source.

insert into public.stops
  (name, slug, category, latitude, longitude, city, state, description,
